# The 200 brightest stars in the far ultraviolet (TD1 1565 A band), excluding the Sun.
#
# Columns: (formal name, other names or None, RA [deg], Dec [deg], spectral type,
#   m(1565) mag, magnitude type ('UV1565'), pm RA * cos(Dec) [mas/yr], pm Dec [mas/yr],
#   parallax [mas])
#
# UV magnitudes: TD1 system, m(1565) = -2.5 log10 F - 21.175, with F the mean flux in
#   erg cm^-2 s^-1 A^-1 over the 1565 A band (FWHM 330 A); zero point of Haynes & Latham (1975),
#   as adopted by the catalog. More negative = brighter. Combined light for multiples
#   closer than the ~1.7' TD1 resolution (e.g. Castor A+B, measured as HD 60178).
# UV fluxes: 178 stars from the TD1 Catalogue of Stellar UV Fluxes (Thompson+ 1978, CDS II/59B).
#   For stars missing there, the band flux was synthesized from TD1 spectra (mean over
#   1400-1730 A) in the UV Bright Star Spectrophotometric Catalogue (Jamar+ 1976, CDS III/39A;
#   18 stars) or its supplement (Macau-Hercot+ 1978, CDS II/86; 4 stars). For the 784 UVBS
#   stars with II/59B m(1565) < 4, synthesized minus II/59B = 0.00 +/- 0.04 mag.
# Known gaps: TD1 recorded no usable data (blended or unobserved) for Alpha1/Alpha2 Crucis
#   (Acrux), Epsilon Centauri and Iota Lupi, which would otherwise rank roughly top-5, top-25
#   and top-100; 22 Orionis and Theta2 Crucis are also unmeasured and might qualify.
# The cutoff falls within a tie at m(1565) = +1.70.
#
# Positions: ICRS / J2000.0 epoch and equinox, from the HYG v4.1 database, which
#   takes them from the Hipparcos catalog (typical accuracy ~1 mas, well under 1").
#   Given here to 1e-6 deg (0.0036"). Matched to TD1 by HD number.
# Proper motions and parallaxes: Hipparcos, the new reduction (van Leeuwen 2007, CDS
#   I/311), matched by HIP number. The proper motion in RA is mu_alpha* = d(RA)/dt *
#   cos(Dec), as catalogs conventionally give it.
# Spectral types: Yale Bright Star Catalog, 5th ed. (fallback: Hipparcos via HYG).
# Formal names: Bayer designation, else Flamsteed, else GCVS variable-star
#   designation, else HR (Bright Star) number.
# Other names: a string or a list of strings, the IAU-approved proper name as listed
#   in HYG v4.1 first, followed by the HR number of a star whose formal name is a
#   variable-star designation.
# Only stars with a Hipparcos entry are included.
#
# Sources: HYG database (CC BY-SA 4.0), github.com/astronexus/HYG-Database;
#   Hipparcos new reduction (CDS I/311);
#   Yale BSC5 (CDS V/50); TD1 catalogs via CDS (cdsarc.cds.unistra.fr).

